<section #sec:section-headings | Section Headings>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A section heading opens a new section. Write one two ways:

Canonical: `<section {args} | Section Title>`
Sigil: `<# {args} | Section Title #>`

<code #code:sec-basic>
<section | Introduction>
The paper begins here.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sec-basic>
<section | Introduction>
The paper begins here.
</minipage>

Headings nest two levels deeper — sub-sections and sub-sub-sections:

Canonical: `<sub-section {args} | Section Title>`
Sigil: `<## {args} | Section Title ##>`

Canonical: `<sub-sub-section {args} | Section Title>`
Sigil: `<### {args} | Section Title ###>`

<code #code:sec-nested>
<section | Materials and Methods>
Opening text.

<sub-section | Sampling>
How samples were drawn.

<sub-section | Instruments>
What we measured with.

<section | Results>
What we found.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sec-nested>
<section | Materials and Methods>
Opening text.

<sub-section | Sampling>
How samples were drawn.

<sub-section | Instruments>
What we measured with.

<section | Results>
What we found.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
Id, #sec:intro
Class, .my-class
numbering-style, arabic | roman | alpha | none
Unnumbered (skip the number), +unnumbered
Unlisted (omit from contents), +unlisted
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

Headings are unnumbered until you switch numbering on with `<config number-sections />`.

`+unnumbered` skips the number *and* leaves the counter untouched, so the next numbered sibling continues unbroken.

`+unlisted` drops the heading and everything nested under it from the contents.

`numbering-style` is per-heading, not global config: it overrides the style for that heading and the ones nested below it (default `arabic`).
</section>
